          "level": 2,
          "content": "11.1 Sources of Light"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.271,
            0.119,
            0.857,
            0.174
          ],
          "content": "The Sun gives out or emits its own light and is the main source of natural light on the Earth. Stars, lightning, natural fire, and certain animals also emit their own light (Fig. 11.1)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.405,
            0.176,
            0.857,
            0.213
          ],
          "content": "In ancient times, humans learnt to create fire — the earliest form of artificial lighting. With"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.405,
            0.213,
            0.648,
            0.284
          ],
          "content": "time, they learnt to create fire using different fuels, such as animal fat, oil, wax, and gas (Fig. 11.2)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.405,
            0.285,
            0.649,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": "With the invention of electricity and different kinds of electric light sources, most of the lighting needs of humans are now met by electric lighting (Fig. 11.3)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.271,
            0.413,
            0.649,
            0.54
          ],
          "content": "Objects that emit their own light are called **luminous** objects. Objects that do not emit their own light are called **non-luminous** objects. The Moon is a  objects. The Moon is a non-luminous object. It does not emit itsnon-luminous object. It does not emit its own light. It just reflects the light emittedown light. It just reflects the light emitted by the Sun that falls on it.by the Sun that falls on it."
        },
